        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: முதுகு",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "முதுகு (mutuku) — \"the back\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malayalam has മുതുക് (mutukŭ), letter for letter until the last one. Telugu takes వీపు (vīpu) and shares nothing with either."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To show somebody your முதுகு (mutuku) is to turn away from them, in Tamil much as in English — and the phrase is meant as unkindly in one language as in the other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "முதுகு (mutuku) is native Dravidian. DEDR 4955 lists Malayalam mutuku."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two: what a garland goes round, and what you turn on somebody."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
