          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: நகம்",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "நகம் (nakam) — \"a nail\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The nail on a finger and the claw on an animal, in one word. Malayalam writes നഖം (nakhaṁ), with a breathed letter in the middle that Tamil's own set does not carry, so Tamil prints it plain: நகம் (nakam). The two words are heard as one and written as two."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu holds on to something else entirely, గోరు (gōru)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "நகம் (nakam) is a loan from Sanskrit nakha, \"nail, claw\" (CDIAL 6914)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four: neck, back, lip, and the hard thing on the end of a finger."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
