          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "எலும்பு (elumbu) — \"a bone\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malayalam എല്ല് (ellŭ) and Telugu ఎముక (emuka) sit either side of Tamil's எலும்பு (elumbu): the same opening, and then three different amounts of word after it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "elumbum tōlum — \"bone and skin\" — is what Tamil says of somebody who has gone very thin. English says the same two things in the other order."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "எலும்பு (elumbu) is native Dravidian. DEDR 839 lists Malayalam elumpu, Kannada elubu and Telugu emuka."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five more of you: neck, back, lip, nail, and what holds the whole lot up."
